import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse('{"title":"Lower call/dimension application to App terms","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Lower `call`/`dimension` application to `App` terms","type":"adr","date":"2026-10-17T00:00:00.000Z","status":"accepted","status_note":"Accepted — implemented; extends the ADR-0147 decline-class retirement to\\nthe application family."},"headers":[],"relativePath":"handl/project/adrs/0148-lower-call-application.md","filePath":"vendored/handl/project/adrs/0148-lower-call-application.md"}'),a={name:`handl/project/adrs/0148-lower-call-application.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="adr-0148-lower-call-dimension-application-to-app-terms" tabindex="-1">ADR-0148: Lower <code>call</code>/<code>dimension</code> application to <code>App</code> terms <a class="header-anchor" href="#adr-0148-lower-call-dimension-application-to-app-terms" aria-label="Permalink to “ADR-0148: Lower call/dimension application to App terms”">​</a></h1><h2 id="context" tabindex="-1">Context <a class="header-anchor" href="#context" aria-label="Permalink to “Context”">​</a></h2><p>ADR-0147 established the pattern: an <code>eval.*</code> scheme whose <code>proj_child</code>/ <code>proj_rest</code> placeholders sit in non-input positions (<code>Seq.snd</code>, <code>Choice</code> arms, <code>Loop.body</code>) cannot be instantiated honestly for the arrow calculus, so the lowerer declines and the runtime falls back to the AST engine. <code>eval.while</code> was retired by a per-kind lowering arm that emits the <code>product</code>/<code>pair_tag</code>/<code>Choice</code> plumbing directly.</p><p><code>eval.call</code> and <code>eval.dimension</code> share the same routing shape:</p><div class="language-"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>Seq[ Seq[ proj_child 0 , route_on_type(&quot;string&quot;) ],</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>     Choice[ call_host(proj_rest) , App(id, proj_rest) ] ]</span></span></code></pre></div><p>— callee in <code>Seq.snd</code>, both <code>proj_rest</code> placeholders in <code>Choice</code> arms: every position non-input, so every <code>f(x)</code> in every <code>.hnl</code> program stayed on the interpreter. Application is the dominant expression form; this decline was the largest single source of interpreter traffic.</p><p>The <code>Choice</code> exists because the <em>env engines</em> must route a string callee to <code>call_host</code> and an arrow callee to <code>App</code>. On the arrow path that routing is unnecessary: <code>Term::App</code> evaluation already classifies the callee value at run time — <code>Str</code> → <code>call_host_fn</code> (module registry), <code>Arrow</code> → <code>apply_app</code>, <code>{__term__, __bound__}</code> record → bound-tuple extension, <code>arr</code>-dispatch object → <code>arr_dispatch</code> — and <code>App.callee</code> / <code>App.args</code> are input-faithful positions already (each arg term runs on the node input). No <code>product</code>/<code>pair_tag</code> plumbing is needed because the discriminator the <code>Choice</code> encodes <em>is</em> the callee value, which <code>App</code> inspects itself.</p><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to “Decision”">​</a></h2><p><code>call</code> and <code>dimension</code> nodes lower through <code>lower_call</code> (<code>crates/handl-kit-arrow-lower/src/lib.rs</code>), a per-kind arm that emits <code>App</code>/<code>Op</code> terms directly:</p><ul><li><strong><code>var</code> callee bound in the compile-time env</strong> — substitute the bound term (quoted via <code>ensure_quoted</code> unless already an arrow value), then apply the lowered arguments: a binding declared with <code>arity &gt; 1</code> consumes its first <code>arity</code> arguments as one tuple <code>App</code> (matching the multi-parameter application contract), with any surplus applied as curried single-argument <code>App</code>s; ordinary bindings apply sequentially (<code>App(App(f, a), b)</code>).</li><li><strong><code>var</code> callee bound as a parameter</strong> — project it out of the input tuple (<code>id</code> for a single parameter, <code>elem &lt;pos&gt;</code> for several), then sequential <code>App</code>s.</li><li><strong><code>var</code> callee unknown to the env</strong> — <code>Op{name, args}</code>: a native-op call, resolved at run time through <code>dispatch_op</code> (covers host ops and <code>op</code> declarations alike).</li><li><strong><code>call</code>-face callee</strong> (a nested <code>call</code>/<code>dimension</code>, per the ADR-0123 face check, falling back to <code>kind == &quot;call&quot;</code> when a language declares no faces) — one <code>App</code> over the whole argument list: the callee produces a bound-arrow record and tuple application extends <code>__bound__</code> flatly, matching the engines&#39; <code>{__term__, __bound__}</code> semantics (<code>Type.rec(m, c0..)(params.., v)</code>).</li><li><strong>any other callee</strong> — sequential <code>App</code>s, preserving curried application for arrow-valued expressions.</li></ul><p><code>eval.call</code>/<code>eval.dimension</code> in <code>eval.yaml</code> stay unchanged: they remain the eval schemes for the env-based engines. The two paths agree because <code>Term::App</code> evaluation performs the same dispatch the <code>Choice</code> encodes — the schemes describe <em>the route</em>, the <code>App</code> term carries it.</p><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to “Consequences”">​</a></h2><ul><li>Application — the dominant expression form — compiles to the variable-free calculus; <code>call</code>/<code>dimension</code> nodes no longer force the AST fallback. The <code>concurrent-epoll</code>/<code>http_rfc</code>/<code>serve-epoll</code> package stack lowers end to end; remaining declines are the intentional ones (self-recursive definitions, <code>return</code>) plus <code>eval.type-annot</code>, whose <code>extend_env</code> arm still needs the env engine.</li><li>The pilot confirms the refined rule: pair plumbing is needed only when a <em>computed</em> discriminator selects between child placeholders (<code>if</code>/<code>ternary</code>/<code>nullish</code>/<code>&amp;&amp;</code>/<code>||</code>/<code>while</code>). When the discriminator is a value the dispatch itself inspects (<code>call</code>), the routing lives in <code>App</code> evaluation and the scheme&#39;s <code>Choice</code> dissolves entirely.</li><li><code>eval.type-annot</code> is the last ordinary decline of the class (<code>Seq[Seq[proj_child 1, extend_env], proj_child 0]</code> — the value child sits in <code>Seq.snd</code>); it declines honestly until it gets the same per-kind treatment.</li></ul>`,13)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};